// INFERENCE 4.3's T-SPAWN-SHARE -- a value reachable from two tasks at once.
//
// Its own file rather than a case in aif_concurrency.psm, and the split is
// forced rather than tidy. The `@elem` key is one per container *type*
// (INFERENCE 3.1, narrowed 2026-08-28 from one per base type), so two lists of
// the same element type still share it -- which is exactly what this file does,
// and one cross-container push then marks every pushed site of that type as
// multiply held. Putting this beside `transferred_is_not_atomic` would drag that
// function's Report to Shared and land it at CrossThread, where it would pass
// while asserting the exact opposite of what it is for.
//
// The base-type version of the same key merged `List<Report>` with every other
// list in the file. That is no longer why the split is needed and the split is
// still needed; keeping the note accurate matters more than keeping it short.
// tests/test_48_aif_shared_elements.psm was split off from test_47 for the same
// reason, and its header measures the cliff.
//
// What has to hold here: the Item reaches CrossThread, and it does so with no
// move error anywhere -- because that is the whole difficulty. Under affine
// references INFERENCE's premise for this rule, "y is still live in the
// parent", is never syntactically true; the move checker rejects any program
// that names the value twice. So the premise is read off the aliasing domain
// instead, and this file is the program that shows why it has to be.

struct Item {
    value: Int
}

fn count_items(xs: List<Item>) -> Int {
    return list_len(xs)
}

// **`kept` is a parameter, and it has to be.** With both containers local the
// Item is *still* CrossThread -- the rule fires correctly -- but it lands at
// **T1**, because SPEC 4.2's T1 clause tests only `E` and a joined task cannot
// outlive the scope its arguments were allocated in. That is the right answer
// and a cheaper one than an atomic count: no count at all, reclaimed by the
// arena reset after the join. It is also INFERENCE 4.1's "this single
// distinction determines whether concurrent code lands at T1 or T4", arriving
// somewhere nobody expected it.
//
// So reaching the atomic path at all takes a value that escapes the region as
// well as the thread. Taking the surviving container from the caller is the
// smallest way to do that, and it is what a real worker pool looks like.
//
// `list_get` reads the element field, so what reaches the second container is
// the first container's Item and not a copy. The move checker sees nothing:
// the operand is a call, not a name, and nothing about a call is a move.
// A-CONTAIN counts containers and makes the element Shared.
//
// Then one container is moved into a task and the other stays here. The Item is
// genuinely reachable from both threads, and nothing in its bindings says so.
fn shared_element_crosses(kept: List<Item>) -> Int {
    let sent: List<Item> = list_new()
    list_push(kept, Item { value: 2 })
    list_push(sent, list_get(kept, 0))

    let t = spawn count_items(sent)
    return join t + list_len(kept)
}

// **The control is tests/test_48_aif_shared_elements.psm, and it cannot live
// here.** It has to be the same sharing with no task -- and putting that
// function in this file would prove nothing, because the `@elem` key is shared
// across the file: both functions' Items would flow through one field node,
// T-REACH would carry Transferred to both, and the control would report
// CrossThread right beside the case it was meant to contrast with. It did,
// while this comment was being written.
//
// test_48 is that program already: two containers, one element, `list_get` in
// between, no spawn anywhere. It reports the Item at **T3 / Isolated / rc** --
// a non-atomic count. Which is what makes the pair meaningful: identical
// aliasing, and the only difference is that one of them hands a container to a
// task.

fn main() -> Int {
    let kept: List<Item> = list_new()
    return shared_element_crosses(kept)
}
